::ALG1_U05_UA_Q01::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_UA_Q01] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.6] Simplify \((3x^2+4x-5)+(2x^2-x+8)\). {
=A. \(5x^2+3x+3\)#Correct. Combine like terms: \(3x^2+2x^2\), \(4x-x\), and \(-5+8\).
~B. \(5x^2+5x+13\)#This adds constants and x-terms incorrectly.
~C. \(6x^4+3x+3\)#Adding polynomials does not multiply exponents.
~D. \(x^2+5x-13\)#This subtracts some terms instead of adding.




}

::ALG1_U05_UA_Q02::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_UA_Q02] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.7] Simplify \((12x^3-8x^2+4x)/(4x)\). {
~A. \(3x^3-2x^2+1\)#Each term must also divide the variable factor x.
~B. \(8x^2-4x\)#This subtracts coefficients instead of dividing each term.
=C. \(3x^2-2x+1\)#Correct. Divide each term by \(4x\).
~D. \(3x^2-8x+4\)#Only the first term was divided correctly.




}

::ALG1_U05_UA_Q03::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_UA_Q03] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.3] Which value equals the square root of 81? {
~A. 8#Since \(8^2=64\), this is not correct.
=B. 9#Correct. \(9^2=81\).
~C. 18#This doubles 9 instead of finding the square root.
~D. 40.5#This halves 81, not square roots it.




}

::ALG1_U05_UA_Q04::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_UA_Q04] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.8] Factor \(x^2+9x+20\). {
~A. \((x-4)(x-5)\)#This gives \(x^2-9x+20\).
~B. \((x+2)(x+10)\)#These factors add to 12, not 9.
~C. \((x-2)(x-10)\)#These factors have the wrong signs and middle term.
=D. \((x+4)(x+5)\)#Correct. 4 and 5 multiply to 20 and add to 9.




}

::ALG1_U05_UA_Q05::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_UA_Q05] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.2] Which equation represents a quadratic function? {
~A. \(y=6x+5\)#This is linear.
=B. \(y=x^2-6x+5\)#Correct. The highest power of x is 2.
~C. \(y=5(2)^x\)#This is exponential.
~D. \(y=5\)#This is constant.




}

::ALG1_U05_UA_Q06::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_UA_Q06] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.3] Solve \(x^2-5x+6=0\). {
=A. \(x=2\) and \(x=3\)#Correct. \((x-2)(x-3)=0\).
~B. \(x=-2\) and \(x=-3\)#These signs would produce a positive middle term.
~C. \(x=1\) and \(x=6\)#These multiply to 6 but do not add to 5.
~D. \(x=-1\) and \(x=-6\)#These roots do not match the factorization.




}

::ALG1_U05_UA_Q07::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_UA_Q07] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.4] What is the vertex of \(y=(x-4)^2+7\)? {
~A. \((-4,7)\)#The sign inside the parentheses is interpreted oppositely.
~B. \((4,-7)\)#The value outside the square is \(+7\), not \(-7\).
=C. \((4,7)\)#Correct. Vertex form gives \((h,k)=(4,7)\).
~D. \((-4,-7)\)#Both signs are incorrect.




}

::ALG1_U05_UA_Q08::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_UA_Q08] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.5] What are the zeros of \(y=(x+2)(x-6)\)? {
~A. \(2\) and \(-6\)#These reverse the signs.
~B. \(2\) and \(6\)#The factor \(x+2\) gives \(-2\).
~C. \(-2\) and \(-6\)#The factor \(x-6\) gives 6.
=D. \(-2\) and \(6\)#Correct. Set each factor equal to 0.




}

::ALG1_U05_UA_Q09::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_UA_Q09] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.6] A model gives side-length solutions \(-5\) and \(8\). Which solution is reasonable? {
=A. \(8\), because length cannot be negative#Correct. The negative value is outside the context for side length.
~B. \(-5\), because it is a solution#Context can reject an algebraic solution.
~C. Both, because quadratics always need two answers#Both algebraic roots are not always meaningful in context.
~D. Neither, because quadratics cannot model length#Quadratic area equations can model side lengths.




}

::ALG1_U05_UA_Q10::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_UA_Q10] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.7] Which model shows 12% growth from an initial amount of 300? {
~A. \(y=300(0.88)^x\)#This shows 12% decay.
=B. \(y=300(1.12)^x\)#Correct. A 12% increase uses a factor of 1.12.
~C. \(y=300+12x\)#This is linear growth by 12 units, not 12%.
~D. \(y=12(300)^x\)#This reverses the initial value and base.




}

::ALG1_U05_UA_Q11::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_UA_Q11] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.8] What is the initial value of \(f(x)=45(0.9)^x\)? {
~A. 0.9#This is the decay factor.
~B. 9#This is not the coefficient or starting value.
~C. 40.5#This is the value after one time period.
=D. 45#Correct. When \(x=0\), the output is 45.




}

::ALG1_U05_UA_Q12::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_UA_Q12] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.9] A medicine amount is multiplied by 0.75 each hour. What does 0.75 mean? {
~A. 75 units are added each hour#The model is multiplicative, not additive.
~B. The initial amount is 0.75#The initial amount would be the coefficient.
=C. 75% remains each hour#Correct. Multiplying by 0.75 means 75% remains.
~D. The amount increases by 75%#A factor below 1 shows decay.




}

::ALG1_U05_UA_Q13::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_UA_Q13] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.6] Which table pattern identifies a linear model? {
=A. Constant first differences#Correct. Linear models have constant rate of change.
~B. Constant second differences#This identifies quadratic behavior.
~C. Constant ratios#This identifies exponential behavior.
~D. No repeated pattern#A model comparison needs evidence of a pattern.




}

::ALG1_U05_UA_Q14::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_UA_Q14] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.7] Which context is best modeled by exponential decay? {
~A. Area of a square#This is quadratic.
~B. A runner travels at constant speed#This is linear.
~C. A rectangle side length is squared#This suggests quadratic structure.
=D. A car loses 15% of its value each year#Correct. Repeated percent decrease is exponential decay.




}

::ALG1_U05_UA_Q15::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_UA_Q15] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.6] Expand \(3x(x-4)\). {
~A. \(3x-12\)#The \(x\) must multiply both terms, including x.
=B. \(3x^2-12x\)#Correct. Distribute \(3x\) to both terms.
~C. \(3x^2-4\)#The second term should be \(-12x\).
~D. \(x^2-12x\)#The coefficient 3 is missing from the first term.




}

::ALG1_U05_UA_Q16::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_UA_Q16] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.8] Factor \(x^2-16\). {
~A. \((x-8)(x+2)\)#These factors do not multiply to \(-16\) with no middle term.
~B. \((x-4)(x-4)\)#This gives \(x^2-8x+16\).
=C. \((x-4)(x+4)\)#Correct. This is a difference of squares.
~D. \((x+4)(x+4)\)#This gives \(x^2+8x+16\).




}

::ALG1_U05_UA_Q17::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_UA_Q17] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.1] Solve \(x(x+7)=0\). {
=A. \(x=0\) and \(x=-7\)#Correct. Each factor can equal zero.
~B. \(x=0\) and \(x=7\)#The factor \(x+7\) gives \(-7\).
~C. \(x=7\) only#This misses \(x=0\) and has the wrong sign.
~D. \(x=-7\) only#This misses \(x=0\).




}

::ALG1_U05_UA_Q18::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_UA_Q18] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.2] A table has first differences 5, 7, 9, 11. What model is suggested? {
~A. Linear#Linear first differences are constant.
=B. Quadratic#Correct. The second differences are constant at 2.
~C. Exponential#Exponential models have constant ratios.
~D. Constant#The outputs are changing.




}

::ALG1_U05_UA_Q19::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_UA_Q19] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.4] What is the axis of symmetry of \(y=(x+3)^2-5\)? {
~A. \(y=-5\)#The axis is vertical, not horizontal.
~B. \(x=3\)#The sign inside is interpreted oppositely.
=C. \(x=-3\)#Correct. The vertex is \((-3,-5)\).
~D. \(y=-3\)#The axis of symmetry is written as \(x=\) a number.




}

::ALG1_U05_UA_Q20::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_UA_Q20] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.6] A ball is launched from the ground and lands at 6 seconds. Which zeros fit? {
~A. \(-6\) and \(0\)#Negative time does not describe landing after launch.
~B. \(3\) and \(6\)#This misses the launch at ground level.
~C. \(-3\) and \(3\)#These do not match the stated landing time.
=D. \(0\) and \(6\)#Correct. Ground level occurs at launch and landing.




}

::ALG1_U05_UA_Q21::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_UA_Q21] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.7] Which function represents exponential decay? {
~A. \(y=80(1.6)^x\)#This represents growth.
~B. \(y=80+0.6x\)#This is linear.
~C. \(y=80x^2\)#This is quadratic.
=D. \(y=80(0.6)^x\)#Correct. The base is between 0 and 1.




}

::ALG1_U05_UA_Q22::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_UA_Q22] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.8] Outputs 4, 12, 36, 108 have what common multiplier? {
=A. 3#Correct. Each output is multiplied by 3.
~B. 4#This is the first output, not the multiplier.
~C. 8#This is the first difference, not the ratio.
~D. 12#This is an output, not the multiplier.




}

::ALG1_U05_UA_Q23::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_UA_Q23] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.9] A population model is \(P(t)=200(1.4)^t\). What does 1.4 represent? {
~A. The initial population#The initial population is 200.
~B. The number of years#The exponent represents time.
=C. The population is multiplied by 1.4 each time period#Correct. This is the growth factor.
~D. The final population#The final population depends on t.




}

::ALG1_U05_UA_Q24::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_UA_Q24] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.6] Which model eventually grows fastest for positive x? {
~A. \(y=100x\)#Linear growth is eventually slower than exponential growth.
=B. \(y=2(3)^x\)#Correct. Exponential growth eventually exceeds linear and quadratic growth.
~C. \(y=x^2\)#Quadratic growth is eventually slower than exponential growth with base above 1.
~D. \(y=500\)#A constant model does not grow.




}

::ALG1_U05_UA_Q25::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_UA_Q25] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.7] Which model type fits a subscription that adds 12 dollars each month? {
=A. Linear#Correct. Adding the same amount each month is linear.
~B. Quadratic#There is no squared relationship.
~C. Exponential#There is no repeated multiplication.
~D. Constant#The total changes each month.




}

::ALG1_U05_UA_Q26::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_UA_Q26] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.3] Which number is a perfect square? {
~A. 20#No integer squared equals 20.
~B. 50#No integer squared equals 50.
~C. 72#No integer squared equals 72.
=D. 49#Correct. \(7^2=49\).




}

::ALG1_U05_UA_Q27::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_UA_Q27] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.7] Simplify \((15x^2+10x)/(5x)\). {
~A. \(10x\)#This subtracts coefficients instead of dividing each term.
=B. \(3x+2\)#Correct. Divide each term by \(5x\).
~C. \(3x^2+2x\)#The variable factor x must also divide out.
~D. \(15x+10\)#The expression was not divided.




}

::ALG1_U05_UA_Q28::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_UA_Q28] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.8] Which factorization matches \(x^2-3x-18\)? {
~A. \((x-6)(x-3)\)#This gives a positive constant.
~B. \((x+6)(x+3)\)#This gives a positive constant and positive middle term.
=C. \((x-6)(x+3)\)#Correct. The factors multiply to \(-18\) and add to \(-3\).
~D. \((x-9)(x+2)\)#This gives middle term \(-7x\).




}

::ALG1_U05_UA_Q29::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_UA_Q29] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.3] What are the zeros of \(y=x^2-4x-12\)? {
=A. \(-2\) and \(6\)#Correct. The function factors as \((x+2)(x-6)\).
~B. \(2\) and \(-6\)#These signs produce the wrong middle term.
~C. \(3\) and \(4\)#These values do not multiply to \(-12\).
~D. \(-3\) and \(-4\)#These values do not match the factorization.




}

::ALG1_U05_UA_Q30::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_UA_Q30] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.5] What is the y-intercept of \(y=x^2-3x+10\)? {
~A. \((10,0)\)#This reverses the intercept coordinates.
=B. \((0,10)\)#Correct. Set \(x=0\), giving \(y=10\).
~C. \((0,-3)\)#The coefficient of x is not the y-intercept.
~D. \((3,0)\)#This is not found by setting \(x=0\).




}

::ALG1_U05_UA_Q31::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_UA_Q31] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.7] A value decreases by 25% each year from 400. Which model fits? {
~A. \(y=400(1.25)^x\)#This is 25% growth.
~B. \(y=400-25x\)#This subtracts a fixed amount, not a percent.
=C. \(y=400(0.75)^x\)#Correct. A 25% decrease leaves 75%.
~D. \(y=25(400)^x\)#This reverses the initial value and base.




}

::ALG1_U05_UA_Q32::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_UA_Q32] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.6] Outputs 2, 5, 10, 17, 26 suggest which model? {
~A. Linear#The first differences are not constant.
~B. Exponential#The ratios are not constant.
~C. Constant#The outputs change.
=D. Quadratic#Correct. The second differences are constant.




}

::ALG1_U05_UA_Q33::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_UA_Q33] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.6] Simplify \(2x(3x+5)\). {
~A. \(6x+10\)#The first term should include \(x^2\).
=B. \(6x^2+10x\)#Correct. Distribute \(2x\) to both terms.
~C. \(5x+7\)#This adds coefficients instead of multiplying.
~D. \(6x^2+5\)#The second term was not multiplied by \(2x\).




}

::ALG1_U05_UA_Q34::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_UA_Q34] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.1] Solve \(x^2+8x+15=0\). {
=A. \(x=-3\) and \(x=-5\)#Correct. \((x+3)(x+5)=0\).
~B. \(x=3\) and \(x=5\)#The roots are negative because the factors are \(x+3\) and \(x+5\).
~C. \(x=-1\) and \(x=-15\)#These multiply to 15 but add to \(-16\).
~D. \(x=1\) and \(x=15\)#These values do not match the middle term.




}

::ALG1_U05_UA_Q35::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_UA_Q35] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.4] For \(y=-2(x-1)^2+6\), what is true? {
~A. It opens upward with minimum \((1,6)\).#The negative coefficient makes it open downward.
~B. It opens upward with maximum \((-1,6)\).#The direction and vertex x-coordinate are incorrect.
~C. It opens downward with minimum \((-1,-6)\).#The vertex is not \((-1,-6)\).
=D. It opens downward with maximum \((1,6)\).#Correct. The coefficient is negative and the vertex is \((1,6)\).




}

::ALG1_U05_UA_Q36::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_UA_Q36] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.8] What is \(f(2)\) for \(f(x)=5(2)^x\)? {
~A. 10#This is \(f(1)\), not \(f(2)\).
~B. 14#This adds instead of using exponentiation.
=C. 20#Correct. \(5(2)^2=20\).
~D. 25#This squares the coefficient instead of the base.




}

::ALG1_U05_UA_Q37::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_UA_Q37] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.7] Which context is best modeled by a quadratic function? {
=A. A ball's height after it is thrown upward#Correct. Projectile height is commonly quadratic.
~B. A population triples each year#This is exponential growth.
~C. A taxi fare increases by 2 dollars per mile#This is linear.
~D. A car loses 10% of its value each year#This is exponential decay.




}

::ALG1_U05_UA_Q38::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_UA_Q38] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.2] A rectangle has sides \(x\) and \(x+7\). Which area function is correct? {
~A. \(A=2x+7\)#This does not multiply side lengths.
~B. \(A=x^2+49\)#This squares each part separately.
~C. \(A=7x\)#This ignores the \(x^2\) term.
=D. \(A=x^2+7x\)#Correct. \(x(x+7)=x^2+7x\).




}

::ALG1_U05_UA_Q39::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_UA_Q39] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.QE.9] A savings model is \(A(t)=1000(1.03)^t\). What percent growth does it show? {
~A. 1%#The factor 1.03 is 3% above 1.
=B. 3%#Correct. \(1.03=1+0.03\).
~C. 30%#A factor of 1.30 would represent 30%.
~D. 103%#The account keeps 100% and gains 3%, not gains 103%.




}

::ALG1_U05_UA_Q40::[Question ID: ALG1_U05_UA_Q40] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.6] Which statement correctly compares model types? {
~A. Quadratic models have constant ratios.#Constant ratios identify exponential models.
~B. Exponential models have constant first differences.#Constant first differences identify linear models.
=C. Linear, quadratic, and exponential models can be distinguished by differences, second differences, and ratios.#Correct. These table patterns identify the model families.
~D. Linear models have constant second differences.#Constant second differences identify quadratic models.




}


